          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Sanctification fundamentally means being made or set apart as holy for God, with mainstream theology commonly emphasizing the believer’s progressive inward renewal; recognized usages also include cultic consecration, definitive status, and honoring God as holy.\",\"book_summary\":\"Sanctification is removal from common status into an owner’s claim for an appointed purpose. God sanctifies by claiming, while people sanctify God or his name by acknowledging and treating him as holy; progressive renewal is treated as a common teaching rather than the defining sense.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"To make or set apart as holy for God.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"To remove from common status and place under an owner’s claim for a designated purpose, or, with God as object, to honor as holy.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book confines God’s sanctifying action primarily to consecrating or claiming rather than identifying sanctification itself with inward moral transformation. It also explicitly distinguishes this from the human act of sanctifying God by honoring him. Thus progressive renewal, central in the consensus’s dominant theological formulation, is reclassified as an associated doctrine or result rather than the term’s core referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction with the consensus’s minimum lexical core of setting apart as holy. The disagreement concerns whether progressive moral renewal belongs within the definition itself or is merely associated with a prior status of consecration and ownership.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus category of holy separation and consecration but sharpens it around ownership, appointed purpose, and the subject-object distinction between God sanctifying creatures and people sanctifying God. These senses are recognizable within the consensus alternatives, so the entry is neither divergent nor novel. The material refinement is its exclusion or reclassification of progressive inward renewal from the defining core, despite that being the consensus’s dominant theological application.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The consensus responses already identify consecration, definitive sanctification, belonging to God, and hallowing God’s name as established senses. Because the book’s principal claims are therefore already represented, one could classify the difference as MATCH and regard its rejection of progressive renewal as only a shift in emphasis. REFINED is preferable because the book’s common-view note and definition materially relocate progressive renewal outside the term’s core extension.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
